31394:/codex-home/skills/k-proof-technique/SKILL.md:21:### Deriving the loop invariant
31395:/codex-home/skills/k-proof-technique/SKILL.md:23:The loop invariant captures what is preserved at the loop head on every iteration. To derive it:
31396:/codex-home/skills/k-proof-technique/SKILL.md:55:3. **Whole-program discharge** — instantiating the loop-invariant claim at the point the entry claim first reaches the loop head must produce exactly the entry claim's postcondition.
31397:/codex-home/skills/k-proof-technique/SKILL.md:244:2. Expose exactly **one constructor layer per circularity**. Give each loop
31398:/codex-home/skills/k-proof-technique/SKILL.md:249:   over the raw constructors**, proved against that loop's circularity, so
31399:/codex-home/skills/k-proof-technique/SKILL.md:254:   sequence unfolded by its own circularity.
31400:/codex-home/skills/writing-spec/SKILL.md:132:A complete spec contains one **entry claim** for the whole program plus one **loop-invariant claim per loop**. The single-loop program used throughout this kit therefore has exactly two claims; a program with two loops needs three, and a nested loop needs its own invariant claim, which the outer loop's proof then uses to step over the inner loop.
31408:/codex-home/skills/proving-spec/SKILL.md:24:  [circularity debugging](../shared/kprove-debug-troubleshoot/circularity-not-applying.md).
31409:/codex-home/skills/proving-spec/SKILL.md:163:The loop-invariant claim in `spec.k` is the loop invariant. When the proof is stuck on the invariant's inductive step, you have two options:
31410:/codex-home/skills/proving-spec/SKILL.md:214:- Prove claims in order: the loop invariant first (`--claims SPEC.loop-inv`), then the whole-program claim. A passing loop invariant makes the whole-program proof fast.
31411:/codex-home/skills/shared/kprove-debug-troubleshoot/circularity-not-applying.md:11:The circularity can be used only when the reached symbolic configuration,
